<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commonwealth of Independent States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1991</span> · Also known as CIS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) is a regional organisation of former Soviet republics, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a> of 8 December 1991 and joined by most other republics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Alma-Ata</a> later that month. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as a framework for cooperation, but with limited powers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> left in 2009 and Ukraine ended its participation in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states.jpg" alt="Commonwealth of Independent States" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Denelson83 at Wikimedia Commons. Redrawn from scratch by MapGrid in 2021. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_CIS.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
